WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.151
Let me show you the developer experience I actually want.

2
00:00:04.151 --> 00:00:09.682
You install one skill, open your application project, and describe the journey that matters.

3
00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:16.398
The skill handles setup, starts the app when necessary, drives the real browser, and saves the evidence.

4
00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:25.880
The important part is what it refuses to do: it can recover harmless UI drift, but it cannot rewrite correctness to manufacture a pass.

5
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:28.878
Installation is one request in Codex.

6
00:00:28.878 --> 00:00:37.868
I point the built-in skill installer at this GitHub directory, and the response confirms that autonomous QA is ready on the next turn.

7
00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:44.122
That copy is self-contained: runtime, dependencies, schemas, and the reviewer UI all travel with the skill.

8
00:00:44.122 --> 00:00:46.467
Now look at the application project.

9
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:49.985
It has only its own dev and reset scripts.

10
00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:56.629
There is no QA dependency, no runner script, and nothing to install for this small demo app.

11
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:10.895
From the separate QA Shop project, I make one request: set up QA, verify checkout, keep the cart, checkout form, and confirmation as required outcomes, save screenshots, and show me the evidence.

12
00:01:10.895 --> 00:01:21.091
The skill discovers the existing dev command, creates a project-specific dot Q A workspace, validates the semantic test, and moves into the native browser.

13
00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:24.489
The YAML is reviewable intent and observable outcomes.

14
00:01:24.489 --> 00:01:30.012
No selector, XPath, coordinate, fixed sleep, or browser script leaks into the application.

15
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.793
Here is the real journey.

16
00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:40.022
The skill starts or reuses the local app, then Codex drives it through the native Browser capability.

17
00:01:40.022 --> 00:01:48.952
The reusable fixture signs in, the agent opens the cart, reaches checkout, submits the approved test payment, and observes the confirmation.

18
00:01:48.952 --> 00:01:54.480
When the run is complete, the packaged workspace opens on the saved result.

19
00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.433
Every expectation passed, the accessible targets are recorded, and the screenshots explain the decision.

20
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:03.835
The developer never operated a separate QA service.

21
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.480
Now I change only the demo application.

22
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:12.588
Proceed to checkout moves into a menu and becomes Continue to payment.

23
00:02:12.588 --> 00:02:18.547
I ask the skill to rerun the last test and keep every expectation unchanged.

24
00:02:18.547 --> 00:02:29.189
The old target is absent, so the agent captures the failure, finds one explicitly equivalent accessible control, retries once, and checks the original checkout-form expectation.

25
00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:30.040
It passes.

26
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.148
That is a healed interaction with before-and-after evidence, not an edited requirement.

27
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.916
Next I switch the application to a real functional bug and use the same rerun prompt.

28
00:02:41.916 --> 00:02:47.267
The checkout interaction works, but the product says the order could not be completed.

29
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:52.618
There is nothing to heal because the action succeeded and the required outcome failed.

30
00:02:52.618 --> 00:02:58.734
The result stays a functional regression, and the original expectation still says order confirmation is visible.

31
00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:01.792
That is the correctness boundary in one screen.

32
00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:06.012
Finally, checkout works but the approved design does not.

33
00:03:06.012 --> 00:03:12.315
The action appears before the heading, and the green success treatment becomes a red warning panel.

34
00:03:12.315 --> 00:03:16.648
The skill runs the separate design checkpoint at its declared viewport.

35
00:03:16.648 --> 00:03:22.951
The result includes the explicit approved reference, the actual screenshot, and concrete order and style findings.

36
00:03:22.951 --> 00:03:24.920
That supports a design regression.

37
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:32.011
It is not the agent grading the page by taste, and it never updates the baseline for me.

38
00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:40.780
Everything the developer keeps is inside the application project: semantic specs, fixtures, results, screenshots, and the last-run pointer.

39
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:48.443
The workspace UI is optional, and its copy buttons produce autonomous-QA prompts instead of leaking runner commands.

40
00:03:48.443 --> 00:04:01.515
So the live loop is simple: install one skill, describe intent, let it own execution and evidence, survive harmless UI churn, and keep real product or design regressions visible.
